3. Hardware
-----------

Current development board:

```text
┌────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Component          │ Specification                                      │
├────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Board              │ LilyGO TTGO T-Display                              │
├────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Microcontroller    │ ESP32 (dual-core Xtensa LX6, Wi-Fi and Bluetooth)  │
├────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Display            │ 1.14 inch IPS TFT, 240 x 135 pixels, ST7789        │
│                    │ controller, RGB565                                 │
├────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Buttons            │ 2 onboard (GPIO 0 and GPIO 35)                     │
├────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Connection         │ USB-C                                              │
└────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Optional sensors (external modules). The T-Display does not include motion or
temperature sensors. To implement the reactive features, external modules
connected over I2C are required:

```text
┌──────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┬────────────┐
│ Function                     │ Suggested module           │ Interface  │
├──────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────┤
│ Accelerometer and gyroscope  │ MPU6050 or MPU9250         │ I2C        │
├──────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────┤
│ Temperature (and pressure)   │ BMP280, BME280 or similar  │ I2C        │
└──────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┴────────────┘
```

Note: sensor choice is subject to hardware verification. The code is being
designed so the sensor driver can be replaced without changing the rest of the
application.

Porting note: the project originally targeted the ESP32-S3. The S3 variant of
the T-Display uses an 8-bit parallel display bus instead of SPI, so pins and
initialization differ. The current code targets the classic ESP32 T-Display
with an SPI display bus.

4. Pin Mapping
--------------

Display (ST7789, SPI):

```text
┌──────────────────────┬────────┐
│ Signal               │ GPIO   │
├──────────────────────┼────────┤
│ SCK (clock)          │ 18     │
├──────────────────────┼────────┤
│ MOSI (data)          │ 19     │
├──────────────────────┼────────┤
│ CS (chip select)     │ 5      │
├──────────────────────┼────────┤
│ DC (data/command)    │ 16     │
├──────────────────────┼────────┤
│ RST (reset)          │ 23     │
├──────────────────────┼────────┤
│ BL (backlight)       │ 4      │
└──────────────────────┴────────┘
```

User input:

```text
┌────────────────┬────────┬────────────────────────┐
│ Button         │ GPIO   │ Function               │
├────────────────┼────────┼────────────────────────┤
│ BOOT           │ 0      │ Change color theme     │
├────────────────┼────────┼────────────────────────┤
│ Right button   │ 35     │ Rotate screen          │
└────────────────┴────────┴────────────────────────┘
```

GPIO 35 is input-only and relies on the board's external pull-up. GPIO 0 uses
the internal pull-up. Do not hold the BOOT button while powering on the board,
since this enters firmware flashing mode.

5. Software and Technologies
----------------------------

- Language: MicroPython
- Firmware: MicroPython for ESP32 (ESP32_GENERIC build recommended)
- Display driver: custom ST7789 routines using machine.SoftSPI
- Graphics: framebuf (RGB565 frame buffers)
- Development tool: Thonny IDE

6. Getting Started
------------------

1. Flash the firmware
   - Download the ESP32_GENERIC build from micropython.org/download.
   - In Thonny, open Tools > Options > Interpreter and select
     MicroPython (ESP32).
   - Click Install or update MicroPython and follow the prompts.

2. Upload the project
   - Clone or download this repository.
   - Open View > Files in Thonny.
   - Copy the project files to the root of the device storage.

3. Run automatically on boot
   - MicroPython executes main.py at startup. Save the entry point on the
     device with exactly that name, in the root directory.
   - To check for startup errors, open the Thonny shell and press Ctrl+D
     to soft-reboot the board.

7. Project Structure
--------------------

The codebase is organized in modules for maintainability. Planned layout:

```text
FriESP/
├── main.py        Entry point: main loop and state handling
├── config.py      Pin mapping, colors, and tunable parameters
├── display.py     ST7789 driver: initialization, windowing, rotation
├── face.py        Face rendering: eyes, mouth, expressions, blinking
├── sensors.py     Temperature and motion sensor abstraction
├── behavior.py    Logic that maps sensor data to expressions
└── README.md
```

The current working prototype (display driver, scrolling banner, rotation and
color buttons) is contained in a single script and will be split into these
modules as development progresses.

8. Display Notes and Known Issues
---------------------------------

- Hardware SPI vs. SoftSPI: during bring-up, the display stayed blank
  (backlight on, no image) with hardware SPI on the installed firmware build.
  Switching to SoftSPI solved it, so the project uses software SPI. It is
  slower, so rendering updates only the regions that change.
- Color inversion: this panel requires the inversion command (0x21) for
  correct colors.
- Byte order: framebuf stores RGB565 in little-endian order while the display
  expects big-endian, so colors are byte-swapped before drawing.
- Memory: large frame buffers can raise MemoryError on an ESP32 without PSRAM.
  Keep text scales moderate.
- Automatic rotation: the T-Display has no motion sensor, so rotation is
  manual (button). It becomes possible after integrating an external
  accelerometer.

The ST7789 RAM is 240 x 320 while the physical panel is 135 x 240, so an
offset is applied to the address window in each orientation:

```text
┌────────────────────────┬──────────┬──────────┬──────────┐
│ Orientation            │ MADCTL   │ X offset │ Y offset │
├────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────┤
│ Landscape              │ 0x60     │ 40       │ 53       │
├────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────┤
│ Portrait               │ 0xC0     │ 53       │ 40       │
├────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────┤
│ Landscape (inverted)   │ 0xA0     │ 40       │ 52       │
├────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┼──────────┤
│ Portrait (inverted)    │ 0x00     │ 52       │ 40       │
└────────────────────────┴──────────┴──────────┴──────────┘
```

